<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- NuGet convention for F# analyzers: FSAC/CLI load them from
         analyzers/dotnet/fs. IncludeBuildOutput=false + the explicit None
         packs below keep the dlls FLAT in that folder (BuildOutputTargetFolder
         alone nests them under net8.0/, one directory below the path every
         setup guide tells users to configure). -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <PackageId>FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers</PackageId>
    <Description>Functional refactoring suggestions (light bulb quick fixes) for F#, built on FSharp.Analyzers.SDK. Over 130 rules covering correctness, performance, idiom and redundancy; every one is Hint severity, so they never gate a build.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.8.11: FR0147 declines an `open` on the mechanism rather than by namespace: a fresh open that brings an extension member named like a method the file calls with a tupled argument - `seen.Contains (e, ct)` under `open System.Linq`, where F# hands the tuple to `Enumerable.Contains(value, comparer)` as two arguments - is noted, never inserted, and the same check holds for a project's own extension namespace (reproduced with a hand-written one: the identical FS0001). The file's tupled calls are collected once and the namespace's extensions only when there is a call to match them against, so an already-open namespace costs nothing. FR0092 leaves a `failwith` alone when a test asserts its exact text - the enrichment would have turned that test red - and under --api-changes enriches it and loosens the assertion to a prefix match in step (FsUnit `should equal` becomes `should startWith`, xUnit `Assert.Equal` becomes `Assert.StartsWith`). FR0015 counts a lambda handed to a List/Seq/Array function as the loop it is (LoopPerf.loopBinders, shared with the other loop rules) and hoists a Regex CONSTRUCTED with a literal pattern out of a loop or such a lambda - `Regex("lit")`, `new Regex("lit", RegexOptions.IgnoreCase ||| RegexOptions.Multiline)`, the qualified spelling - moving the construction's own source text to a `let private xRegex =` binding above the enclosing declaration and replacing only the construction, so a `let regex = Regex(";([a-z]*)=")` followed by `regex.Split(v)` (FSharp.Analyzers.SDK's expandMultiProperties, hoisted by hand in commit 6dd6679) becomes `let regex = azRegex` with the Split untouched; declined for a non-literal pattern, options naming a local or a multi-line construction, where FR0037's "Regex built in a loop" note keeps firing - and stands down where FR0015 fixes. The hoisted binding lands above a plain `//` comment block describing the declaration as well as above its `///` doc, never between either and the `let`. --api-changes now rewrites call sites in the SIBLING PROJECTS of the same solution: a public tupled function (FR0090) or data-first pair (FR0091) is reshaped together with its calls in every project of the run that references it - the test project, typically - each such project typechecked against the library sources in memory, its uses matched by declaration and its files edited in the same atomic set as the definition, then typechecked again before the edits stay; a referencing C# or VB project, a sibling that does not typecheck, a script #r-ing the built assembly, or a bare project run with no solution above it that lists it holds every public function in its present shape and says so once per project. An internal function of an assembly with InternalsVisibleTo friends now waits the same way for every friend to be read, where before the friend was never consulted. A cross-file suggestion that could apply in one file but not another is put back whole rather than left half-applied. 0.8.10: FR0085 no longer strips a `new` whose bare name would be captured by a FUNCTION. `new string(chars, i, n)` became `string(chars, i, n)`, which is FSharp.Core's conversion applied to a TUPLE and yields the literal "(System.Char[], 1, 12)" - management-portal's id generator handed that out as every id and its uniqueness test failed, all of it typechecking cleanly so no build check could see it. Three guards now: FSharp.Core's conversion names, a value declared in the type's own module, and any function an `open` in the file brings into scope; each was reproduced on a running probe before being fixed. FR0029's tail extraction ADVISES by default and fixes only under { "FR0029": { "extractTail": true } }: `runTail` names no business idea, and a smaller resumable body is not a goal in itself - only FS3511 makes it one, and FS3511 is emitted at codegen where no analyzer can see it. Hoisting plain lets and splitting branches move code that already existed, name nothing new, and stay fixes. The return hoist gained three guards of its own, each measured rather than assumed: a `use` inside the branch (the builder's Using becomes the language's, DisposeAsync silently becoming Dispose), a directive block opening on the line below it (another configuration may hold arms this one cannot see), and the emit no longer leaves `return` alone on its line or duplicates a trailing brace. A tail closing on a bare `return` now extracts as a plain closure instead of gaining a second task builder. New knob { "FR0029": { "hoistReturnOnAsync": true } } extends ONLY the return hoist to async, worth about 27 percent less generated IL on a twelve-arm branch though identical on time and allocation. Rule knobs now read a JSON bool as happily as 0 or 1.
 FR0029's tail extraction now waits for the COMPILER: FS3511 is emitted at codegen where no analyzer can see it, but the apply tool builds before it rewrites and the warning carries the builder's own position, so those lines are handed to the rule and `runTail` is offered where the dynamic fallback is real. Absent that warning the bar is `tailLines`, raised from 10 to 40 - a shape unwieldy on its own merits, since a smaller resumable body is not a goal in itself. Below the bar the rule now says NOTHING: a note about code that has no problem helps nobody, so it is a quickfix or silence. Hoisting plain lets and splitting branches move code that already existed, name nothing new, and stay fixes either way. FR0065 gained two offers beside the Tls12 swap - comment the dead protocol out of the flags keeping the live one, or comment the whole setting out and let the OS negotiate - and `{ "FR0065": { "dropLegacyProtocols": true } }` lets an unattended run apply the first; `--api-changes` is deliberately NOT the gate, since retiring a protocol changes what the wire negotiates rather than what callers compile against. Protocol detection now reads `[&lt;Obsolete&gt;]` beside the curated list, because the framework marks its own inconsistently: SslProtocols.Tls11 carries the attribute and SecurityProtocolType.Tls11 - the one real code sets - does not, a decade after TLS 1.1 was deprecated. FR0028 no longer reports the cure as the disease: `let chunked = Array.chunkBySize 200 ids` followed by `for batch in chunked` is deliberate batching, and the guard had only ever looked inside the loop header. FR0015 rewrites a literal-pattern `Regex.Replace(s, "abcd", "x")` to `s.Replace("abcd", "x")`, declining wherever the two differ - measured: a `$` in the replacement is substitution syntax to one and text to the other, and an empty pattern makes String.Replace throw where Regex inserts between every character.
 0.8.8: a fix can be applied to a standalone script at last. The .fsx safety net asked whether the REWRITTEN script still had a typecheck context, and readScript answers None both for a script carrying any error and for one with no #load at all - so the rollback condition was already true before any edit, and every fix to such a script was applied and put straight back, on every run, forever. It now compares against the state BEFORE the edit, so only a script this pass actually broke may veto its group. A connection string whose server is the loopback host is no longer reported as a leak whatever its password looks like - localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, (local), (localdb) and a bare dot, each allowing an instance or port suffix - which is a far better signal than guessing at the password's shape. A credential in a [&lt;Literal&gt;] cannot move to configuration, since a literal is a compile-time constant, so FR0127's remedy does not apply to it: FR0153 says the value should be a DEVELOPMENT credential instead, as an advisory note rather than a warning. FR0144 offers #r "nuget: Id, Version" where the package is not on disk at all, which is what paket's storage:none leaves, reading the version off the path when the layout carries one and no lock file otherwise; only where nothing local can be re-pointed to, so a path that can be aimed at a debug build keeps its advantage, and never for a net4x asset whose fsi.exe has no package-reference resolution. Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions is pinned to 8.0.0: the 6.0.0 that FSharp.Analyzers.SDK brings in transitively lost to the 8.0.0.0 a SQLProvider type provider asks for, its MySqlConnector failed to initialise, and a project whose schema comes from one reported hundreds of errors before any fix and was refused outright. The rule descriptions moved out of the README into Rules.md, which now carries the quick table and a section per rule. 0.8.7: FR0102 stops firing on a receiver the loop rebinds on every iteration. It asks LoopPerf.loopBinders what a match arm binds, and that reads Text.patBoundNames, which answered with NO names at all for four pattern forms - named union-case fields (Field = pat), cons patterns (a :: rest), record patterns and optional parameters - because an unhandled form fell through a silent catch-all rather than failing. Every list destructured out of a match arm therefore looked loop-invariant. On this repository that was 84 notes, all of them false, now none; the tell was that the advice it gave, bind the length once outside the loop, is impossible where the binder is fresh each iteration. RecursiveAppend, Reraise and QualifiedNames read the same helper and see those binders now too. QualifiedNames asks FCS for the project referenced assemblies once per project rather than once per file. 0.8.4: FR0121 quiet on expression-tree translator arms, FR0102 bounded indexes, FR0032 names a disposable base, FR0055 respects an acknowledging comment and keeps the log receiver in scope with the IO-only catch limited to single-call bodies, FR0065 offers SHA-256 for MD5, FR0126 offers the Process argument list; 0.8.3: FR0147 (repeated qualified names become an open), FR0146 (unparametrized SQL), a priority flag on the likely-defect rules, notes only review mode, workspace targets, HTML and CSV reports, editor quick-fix offers for FR0032, FR0038, FR0046, FR0047, FR0055, FR0061, FR0062, FR0079, FR0089 and FR0105, FR0066 widened to helpers and one-hop strings, FR0120 and FR0124 across Microsoft.Extensions.Logging, Serilog and Logary, FR0127 connection strings and tokens, FR0075 and FR0028 false positives removed, alternative fixes as separate messages; 0.8.2: FR0130 (Literal), FR0133 (backtick names) and FR0016 (Struct) edit the companion signature file in step, through the mechanism FR0022 introduced: one atomic edit set spanning the .fs and the .fsi, or a withheld fix where the signature cannot be read; private declarations are unaffected. FR0130 and FR0133 are on by default again. FR0004 judges the callback of a moved operation rather than the whole enclosing function, and moves nothing over a module-level or captured collection under a callback. FR0074 checks the flattened path head against every module, namespace and type in scope. FR0085 keeps new where a type name is split across namespace fragments of different assemblies. FR0095 reads scope rather than the whole file. The shape-changing rules share one signature-file gate. 0.7.5: a capability fix now stands down where it has nowhere safe to live. On a pass over a framework wider than the project narrowest target, such a fix belongs inside the project own framework guard or nowhere: where the project offers no framework-shaped constant, FR0038, FR0039 and FR0106 keep their advice and drop the edit rather than going in plainly and being reverted by the all-frameworks build - which took every innocent fix in those files with it, 62 applied and 7 files put back on SwaggerProvider. FR0119 asks the same question in full: its gate read only the case where a guard exists that its multi-edit swap cannot pair with, and missed the case of no guard at all, so it took Dispose to DisposeAsync on a project whose netstandard2.0 target has no such interface. Re-sweeping that repository now applies 59 fixes, keeps every one, and leaves every target framework building. 0.7.4: fixes from sweeping SQLProvider, which the tool left with a broken build. FR0140 parenthesises the value it moves into a construction: a cast binds looser than the named property, so cmd.Connection then con cast to SqlConnection folded to an equality against an undefined Connection. The all-frameworks arbiter now counts errors both ways rather than asking pass or fail - a project can be broken for two reasons at once, and a pre-existing breakage outside the snapshot made every fix look innocent and put them all back. The generated-file sniff reads thirty lines and knows the prose and attribute forms, not just the XML marker, so a FAKE-written AssemblyInfo is left alone. The Visual Studio extension checks the file rather than trusting the buffer content type: F# sources only, and never a vendored, package or generated path. --parse-only no longer reads framework-conditional DefineConstants, which activated one framework branch and hid the rest. FR0039 pairs with the project own framework guard like FR0038 does, so a StringComparison overload no longer breaks netstandard2.0; FR0119 keeps its advice but withholds the edit where a guard would be needed, its rewrite spanning more than one edit. 0.7.3: FR0141 stays silent inside async and task, where the advice does not hold: a task compiles to a resumable state machine and recursive return! grows the stack, and in async the recursive function must return an Async so the change reaches the signature rather than the loop. FR0141 notes a while loop that carries state forward by mutation and leaves through a boolean flag - tail recursion written inside out. Deliberately not the search loop: a carried value whose every assignment is a counter bump is an index, and that shape already short-circuits and allocates nothing (measured 12x faster than Array.exists on an early hit). Note only. FR0107 documented accurately: it already folds a pure let-prefix into the lambda, which the rule text denied. 140 rules. FR0140 folds property assignments that immediately follow a construction into F#'s named-property construction - not a constructor overload and not faster, the same calls in the same order, but the object reads as constructed rather than assembled and the half-built value stops being nameable. Only the uninterrupted run straight after the binding folds in. 139 rules. FR0089 no longer reads the F# 6 indexer spelling as a single-tuple list. Since F# 6, grid[0, 1, 2] parses as an ATOMIC application of a bracket to the thing before it, the same shape as the [ 1, 2 ] paste trap the rule hunts, so every multi-dimensional index raised a false correctness note. The atomic flag is what separates them, and a spaced list argument stays flagged. Found by surveying an agent-generated corpus, where all six of the rule notes in one test file were this and none a real trap. Both index spellings are now locked into the tests across the rule set: every rule that DETECTS indexing already matched the atomic form beside the legacy DotIndexedGet node, and the rest only traverse into brackets. SARIF reports now name the URL the package and --help publish. 0.7.2: FR0139 swaps Seq functions to the Array module where the typed tree proves an array — measured on .NET 10, arrays only, with `contains` on int arrays defaulting to vectorised Enumerable.Contains and offering Array.contains beside it. Rule fixes found by dogfooding and by re-sweeping the corpus: FR0030 no longer emits code that cannot parse for a range source, FR0119 binds only on a computation expression's own statement spine (21 rollbacks in one repo), FR0029 tail extraction stops at a `use` and FR0005 cures the shape an older version wrote, FR0039 keeps backticked receivers, and a failed verification no longer leaves kept fixes suppressed. The benchmarks now target .NET 10. 0.7.1: the published package now reliably carries BOTH analyzer SDK builds flat in analyzers/dotnet/fs — FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide.dll for stock Ionide (SDK 0.35.0) and the 0.37.2 build for the CLI and newer FsAutoComplete — plus the review-hardening fixes 0.7.0's rolling pack accumulated; the repository gains a classic Visual Studio extension (VSIX): squiggles and light-bulb fixes in VS 2022-2026 rendered from an FsAutoComplete sidecar. 0.7.0: cross-file migrations arrive — under --api-changes, strictly internal declarations now migrate project-wide with every use classified against its own file (FR0069 voption, FR0093 struct tuples, FR0049 taskify), gated hard: public never, InternalsVisibleTo assemblies never, one unconvertible use vetoes everything. FR0029 grows branch-return tail extraction and per-arm match/match! splitting into nested tasks, and match! arm interiors are finally visible to every rule (an SDK walker hole). Solution runs order narrowest target first and shared directive-free files sweep once across all frameworks and projects; fully-swept compilations skip their typecheck. New rules: FR0132 trailing comments promote to XML docs; FR0133 five-word test names become double-backtick sentences (locals opt-in); FR0134 UtcNow-fed DateTime fields migrate to DateTimeOffset (opt-in); FR0135 markdown-bearing script comments become literate (** cells. FR0022 also mines field names from XAndY case names and clear trailing comments. Everything adversarially audited: keyword field names, fake-clock shadowing, cross-type comparisons, multi-line plain strings under re-indentation, TestCaseSource string references and more are all vetoed by construction. FR0136 states zero-argument Guid constructors as Guid.Empty (the editor also offers NewGuid()); FR0137 fuses consecutive same-module map passes via composition; FR0035 gains the module-level-set quick-fix (in-place Set.ofList when probes are the only uses, a HashSet companion otherwise); FR0039 extends its fix to the method-call shape (path.ToLower().StartsWith "file:" becomes the StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase overload, per the .NET string best-practices guide); FR0005 collapses no-op return! task wrappers and unjustified tail thunks; the multi-pass loop warns on non-convergence and blocks a rule that keeps growing the same file, and a failed verification now rolls back only the fixes at the error sites instead of every fix in the file. FR0138 rewrites hand-rolled emptiness tests (isNull x || x = "") to String.IsNullOrEmpty/IsNullOrWhiteSpace; FR0139 swaps Seq functions to the Array module where the typed tree proves an array (measured 2.3x on exists, enumerator allocation gone) - arrays only, and never the numeric aggregates that FR0041 sends to vectorised LINQ. 138 rules. 0.6.8: fixes first — a run now prints its fixes and holds advisory notes to one summary line (--notes lists them, --report exports SARIF). FR0029 applies its own FS3511 advice: hoisting pre-await lets (documenting comments travel along), splitting branching tasks per arm, wrapping non-awaiting tails, extracting awaiting try/finally suffixes; the multi-pass loop chains the moves. FR0049 rewrites blocking binds (GetAwaiter().GetResult(), .Result, Async.RunSynchronously) to let!/do! in task and async alike; sync-direction swaps stay config-opt-in. Suppression policy (suppressions: all/no-correctness/none plus --honor-suppressions) and per-rule config parameters. New rules FR0117-FR0131: or-pattern arm folding; CancellationToken omission and None-propagation; awaitable-twin calls; catch blocks logging without the exception (PII-aware); DateTime.Now/UtcNow.Date pitfalls; invalid regexes; Monitor.Enter/Exit to lock; interpolated logging to message templates; invisible/bidi Unicode with escape fixes; process-execution sinks; provider-format API-key literals; obsolete crypto constructors to static factories; guard-equality clauses to literal patterns; module constants gain [&lt;Literal&gt;]; provably tail-recursive functions gain [&lt;TailCall&gt;]. match/match!/function parity for arm rules; SHA1 editor alternatives on FR0065. 130 rules.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <Authors>Tuomas Hietanen</Authors>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>fsharp;analyzers;refactoring;codefix;ionide;fsautocomplete</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Unlicense</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <!-- analyzers are build-time tools: nothing flows to the consumer's output -->
    <DevelopmentDependency>true</DevelopmentDependency>
    <!-- analyzer packages intentionally have no lib/ folder -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <!-- the same analyzers built against FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.35.0 — the
         version stock Ionide's FsAutoComplete bundles. The SDK loads only
         analyzers built against its own version and skips the rest with a
         log line, so shipping both assemblies side by side lets every host
         pick the one it can use: stock Ionide loads the .Ionide.dll, the
         CLI and newer FsAutoComplete load the main one. -->
    <None
      Include="..\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide\bin\$(Configuration)\net8.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide.dll"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net8.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.dll"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net8.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.xml"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- build ordering only: the packed .Ionide.dll must exist -->
    <ProjectReference
      Include="..\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide.fsproj"
      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
      PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

